> requiring reads in order for writes to complete on the same side of the pipe

The reads and writes in question here are both on the same side of the pipe?  If so, I would not expect writes to be blocked on reads then, assuming the pipe was created for overlapped I/O.  A repro demonstrating that isolated aspect would be needed; we have lots of tests that I'd expect to fail if that weren't working.  A write on one side of the pipe may need a read on the other side in order for the write to be unblocked (asynchronously), but a write on one side of the pipe shouldn't require a read on the same side of the pipe in order for the write to be unblocked.

There could of course be a secondary synchronization mechanism at play, e.g. if the server doesn't read until data its written is read, then that could manifest as the client deadlocking in a write without a client read, since the client's read might be necessary to unblock the server's write only after which the server would read to unblock the client's write, e.g. this will likely deadlock:
```C#
using System.IO.Pipes;

string name = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
var server = new NamedPipeServerStream(name, PipeDirection.InOut, 1, PipeTransmissionMode.Byte, PipeOptions.Asynchronous);
var client = new NamedPipeClientStream(".", name, PipeDirection.InOut, PipeOptions.Asynchronous);

await Task.WhenAll(server.WaitForConnectionAsync(), client.ConnectAsync());
Console.WriteLine("connected");

_ = Task.Run(async () =>
{
    await client.WriteAsync(new byte[1]);
    await client.ReadAsync(new byte[1]);
});

await server.WriteAsync(new byte[1]);
await server.ReadAsync(new byte[1]);
```
If you switch the order for either client or the server to read first, then it no longer deadlocks.  It's effectively a lock inversion.
